Noah: Heads-up 9 I opened a tiny docs PR that updates our SDK examples to use a consistent streaming iterator pattern and a short retries section. Preview: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/3710. Looking for wording + code sanity check across py/ts/go.

Greta: Nice. Quick scan: examples look cleaner. Python uses a sync iterator now instead of callbacks, e.g.:```python
for frame in client.chat.stream(req):
    if frame.pause_hint:
        # optional: caller-level backoff
        time.sleep(0.5)
        continue
    print(frame.text, end='')
```
We should say "pause_hint" is advisory only.

Hassan: In TS I made the example explicit about not conflating streaming cursors with pagination tokens:```ts
// realtime stream of deltas
for await (const delta of client.chat.stream(req)) {
  if (delta.error?.code === 'RATE_LIMIT') {
    // surface to caller so they can retry with backoff
    continue
  }
  updateUi(delta.text)
}

// separate: fetch paged history
let resp = await client.history.list({page_token: undefined})
while (resp) { process(resp.items); resp = resp.next_token ? await client.history.list({page_token: resp.next_token}) : null }
```
Add tiny sentence: "Streams = ordered deltas, Pagination = snapshot pages."

Noah: Good call. Also added a short cross-language pseudocode block for exponential backoff (keeps examples DRY). Idea: show backoff as best-practice, not required by the SDK.

Greta: Go sample currently uses a Receive() loop; I refactored to return an iterator-like channel but added comments to respect ctx:```go
stream, err := client.Stream(ctx, req)
if err != nil { return err }
for {
  chunk, ok := stream.Recv()
  if !ok { break }
  if chunk.Err != nil {
    // caller decides: log, backoff, or return
    continue
  }
  fmt.Print(chunk.Text)
}
```
Note: explicitly call out "do not spawn unbounded goroutines per stream".

Hassan: Found two small link anchors in the migration guide that pointed to `#old-streaming-api`. I pushed a patch to replace with `#streaming-patterns` and added a one-line redirect comment.

